VDI18N.add('en', 'notes/celestial-sphere', [
"The Sky and the Celestial Sphere: teaching notes",
"The Sky and the Celestial Sphere",
"Intro astronomy (non-majors and majors); Astro {#} Topic {#}",
"The sky behaves like a sphere turning once a day about an axis through Earth's poles. Altitude and azimuth say where to look from one place at one moment; right ascension and declination are fixed on the sphere, like longitude and latitude.",
"The north celestial pole stands as many degrees above the northern horizon as your latitude. Polaris is within {#}° of it today.",
"Stars within that many degrees of the pole never set (circumpolar); stars as close to the other pole never rise. A star on the celestial equator is up for half of each day, everywhere.",
"The Sun moves along the ecliptic, tilted {#}° to the celestial equator, so its noon height changes by {#}° from June to December. The Moon moves about {#}° east among the stars each day, within {#}° of the ecliptic.",
"Precession turns Earth's axis around a cone once in about {#} years, so the pole star and the constellation of the March equinox change.",
"Students need only latitude and longitude on Earth and the idea that Earth spins. The demo pairs well with Stellarium Web (free, in the browser): Stellarium shows the real sky; this demo shows the sphere from outside so students can see why the sky moves as it does. The demo opens paused on A winter night at Ruston. Turn on <strong>Projector</strong> for a large room; the Flat outside view is lighter on old laptops than 3D.",
"<strong>A winter night at Ruston.</strong> Press <strong>Play</strong> at {#} min/s. In the left view every star turns about the axis through the observer; in the right view stars rise in the east and set in the west, and the stars near Polaris circle it. Point out that the axis is tilted by {#}° minus the latitude (Q1).",
"<strong>North Pole, then On the equator.</strong> At the pole the celestial equator lies on the horizon and nothing rises or sets; at the equator the axis lies along the horizon and every star is up {#} hours (Q3).",
"<strong>Southern sky, Sydney.</strong> The south celestial pole is {#}° up with no bright star near it; Crux and the pointers never set; Polaris never rises (Q2).",
"<strong>June solstice Sun, December solstice Sun, Midnight Sun.</strong> Read the highest altitude of the Sun each time (Q4). Turn on <strong>Always up and never up zones</strong> to show the circumpolar caps.",
"<strong>The Moon through a month.</strong> One day each second at the same clock time: the Moon moves east among the stars, waxes and comes back to the same stars in {#} days (Q5).",
"<strong>Pole star in {#} BC, Precession of the pole.</strong> The sky circles Thuban at Giza; then {#} years a second in 3D shows the axis sweeping its cone (Q6).",
"<strong>Antipodes: Madrid and New Zealand.</strong> Madrid at {#}:{#} on the December solstice: the Sun {#}° down. Ask Q7, then press <strong>Go to the antipode</strong> in The antipode: {#}° S, {#}° E, {#}:{#} the next morning, the Sun {#}° up, the Moon down. The whole sky view shows the half of the sky that was under Madrid's horizon. <strong>Go back</strong> returns.",
"<strong>Q1.</strong> Predict: how high above the northern horizon is Polaris seen from Ruston, at {#}° N? Select Polaris in A winter night at Ruston and check. Why is it not exactly {#}°?",
"<strong>Q2.</strong> From Ruston, is Kochab (declination {#}°) circumpolar? Is Capella (declination {#}°)? From Sydney, at {#}° S, does Acrux, in the Southern Cross (declination −{#}°), ever set?",
"<strong>Q3.</strong> On the equator, where on the horizon does Mintaka, almost exactly on the celestial equator, rise? How long is it above the horizon each day?",
"<strong>Q4.</strong> Find the Sun's highest altitude at Ruston on June {#} and on December {#}. Explain the difference using the tilt of Earth's axis. Why does the Sun not set at Tromsø, at {#}° N, in late June?",
"<strong>Q5.</strong> Using The Moon through a month, how far east among the stars does the Moon move from one evening to the next? How many days until it is back among the same stars?",
"<strong>Q6.</strong> Which star was near the north celestial pole when the pyramids at Giza were built, about {#} BC? Which will be near it in AD {#}? About when does the March equinox point leave Pisces for Aquarius?",
"<strong>Q7.</strong> At some moment the Sun is {#}° below the horizon in Madrid ({#}° N, {#}° W) and the Moon is {#}° up. Where on the Earth is Madrid's antipode? What are the Sun's and the Moon's altitudes there at the same moment, and why does the Moon not come out at exactly −{#}°? What season is it there?",
"<strong>\"Polaris is the brightest star in the sky.\"</strong> It is about the 48th brightest. What makes it special is its place within {#}° of the north celestial pole.",
"<strong>\"The stars are fixed; only the Sun moves.\"</strong> The whole sky turns once a day because Earth turns. Stars also rise about {#} minutes earlier each night, so the evening sky changes through the year.",
"<strong>\"The Sun is straight overhead at noon.\"</strong> At Ruston the Sun is never overhead: its highest is {#}° in June. It can be overhead only between {#}° S and {#}° N.",
"<strong>\"The pole star is forever.\"</strong> Precession moves the pole: Thuban {#} years ago, Polaris now, Vega in about {#} years.",
"<strong>\"Star signs match where the Sun is.\"</strong> The dates of the signs were set about {#} years ago; precession has since shifted the Sun's place by almost a whole constellation.",
"<strong>\"People at the antipode see the same stars upside down.\"</strong> They see the other half of the sky: whatever is below your horizon is above theirs, at the same angle.",
"Star places: the Yale Bright Star Catalogue (Hoffleit and Warren {#}, CDS V/{#}), {#} stars to V = {#}, J2000 positions moved along their proper motions. Constellation boundaries: IAU (Delporte {#}), looked up as tabulated by Roman ({#}).",
"Precession of the equator and the ecliptic: Vondrák, Capitaine and Wallace ({#}), valid for ±{#} years. The Earth turns by the Earth rotation angle (IERS Conventions {#}). Nutation (up to {#}″) and the aberration of starlight for the stars (up to {#}″) are left out.",
"Sun: Meeus, Astronomical Algorithms, ch. {#} (about {#}°). Moon: Meeus ch. {#} with the SOFA moon98 coefficients (about {#}″), seen from the observer's place on the WGS84 ellipsoid. ΔT from Espenak and Meeus ({#}).",
"Highest and lowest altitude in a day: {#}° − |φ − δ| and |φ + δ| − {#}°. No refraction: in the real sky the air lifts objects at the horizon by about {#}°, so real days are a few minutes longer and Polaris is a little higher near the horizon.",
"Eclipses: the two disks seen from the observer (solar); the Moon against the Earth's shadow with the Explanatory Supplement ({#}) radii (lunar). Contact times agree with NASA's within about a minute (Dallas totality, April {#}, {#}: {#}:{#}:{#} to {#}:{#}:{#} UT here, {#}:{#}:{#} to {#}:{#}:{#} UT in NASA's tables).",
"Tested against ERFA (precession, sidereal time, Moon), astropy (constellations) and NASA eclipse times in curriculum/validation/test_celestial-sphere.cjs.",
"The antipode of (φ, λ) is (−φ, λ ± {#}°); this holds for geodetic latitude on the ellipsoid too, which is symmetric through the center. Its vertical is opposite yours and its north is the same direction, so a star at altitude h, azimuth A here is at −h, {#}° − A there. The Sun and Moon are worked out from the antipode itself, so the Moon's {#}° parallax shows: the two altitudes add to slightly less than zero.",
"Pair with the Astro {#} activity Reading the Sky with Stellarium: find the same stars in Stellarium Web and compare altitude, azimuth, right ascension and declination.",
"Homework: from a dark spot, estimate the altitude of Polaris with a fist at arm's length (about {#}°) and compare with your latitude."
]);
